00:00:45.653 --> 00:00:58.356
I don’t know if you can tell this&nbsp;
yet, but you’re looking at a killer.

00:00:58.356 --> 00:00:59.880
Its body&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.880 --> 00:01:02.720
is long with many threads, and it seems more like&nbsp;
a crack on the screen than a living organism.

00:01:02.720 --> 00:01:10.960
Things may seem quiet and calm, but along&nbsp;
its body, the killer wears its victims.

00:01:10.960 --> 00:01:13.680
It would be understandable&nbsp;
though if you miss that part.

00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:19.560
Those victims have been so engulfed from&nbsp;
within by their enemy, they look more like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.560 --> 00:01:26.200
unusual leaves or bits of debris. Not an&nbsp;
animal, swimming around the microcosmos.

00:01:26.200 --> 00:01:49.600
And certainly not a rotifer, caught&nbsp;
in the strands of a predacious fungus.

00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:53.040
If you have been following Journey&nbsp;
to the Microcosmos for some time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.040 --> 00:01:55.200
this might sound like a familiar story.

00:01:55.200 --> 00:01:58.680
A few years ago, we featured&nbsp;
a horror story of sorts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.680 --> 00:02:03.480
starring a fungus that spins a web to trap worms.

00:02:03.480 --> 00:02:08.360
So consider this a proper slasher&nbsp;
movie sequel. You don’t need to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.360 --> 00:02:11.400
have seen the original to know what’s happening.

00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:16.240
But we hope that, when you’re done watching&nbsp;
rotifers suffer untimely ends under strange&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.240 --> 00:02:24.720
circumstances, you’ll want to go back and&nbsp;
watch nematodes suffer a similar fate.

00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:27.360
Sequels are of course tricky things,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:31.400
and you run the challenge of&nbsp;
treading a lot of the same ground.

00:02:31.400 --> 00:02:35.360
Of course, we’ll have plenty of&nbsp;
violence and death, but fortunately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.360 --> 00:02:41.360
this rotifer-packed sequel brings something&nbsp;
else to the table that the nematodes didn’t:

00:02:42.400 --> 00:02:43.520
sex.

00:02:43.520 --> 00:02:51.040
But first, the violence and death.

00:02:51.040 --> 00:02:56.960
There are at least 60 species of fungi that&nbsp;
we know of that like to attack rotifers.

00:02:56.960 --> 00:03:02.640
Much of what we know about these species&nbsp;
comes from scientists using rotifers as bait.

00:03:02.640 --> 00:03:08.600
They take a sample of rotifers and then add a&nbsp;
bit of soil to see if the fungus is in there.

00:03:08.600 --> 00:03:10.540
And then they watch.

00:03:10.540 --> 00:03:16.320
It sounds a little dark when we phrase it that&nbsp;
way, but of course here we are, watching this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.320 --> 00:03:23.720
Zoophagus fungus that was growing inside of a&nbsp;
humidity chamber as it eats away at a rotifer.

00:03:23.720 --> 00:03:30.120
From a distance, the fungus seems so dull&nbsp;
and uninteresting. It’s not even moving.

00:03:30.120 --> 00:03:37.400
Except, of course, that it is. Inside the&nbsp;
fungus is a flurry of activity, as nutrients&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.400 --> 00:03:45.120
and molecules stream through the cytoplasm and&nbsp;
across the vast expanses of this predator’s body.

00:03:45.120 --> 00:03:48.120
This rotifer has been caught by a fungus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.120 --> 00:03:53.000
flopping from side to side while&nbsp;
the fungus seems to do nothing.

00:03:53.000 --> 00:03:57.150
An animal that can swim up&nbsp;
against an organism that can’t…

00:03:57.150 --> 00:03:59.960
it shouldn’t be a fight.

00:03:59.960 --> 00:04:07.000
But the rotifer is stuck, caught on&nbsp;
adhesive pegs that line the fungus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.000 --> 00:04:10.360
and make it impossible for the rotifer to escape.

00:04:10.360 --> 00:04:18.720
And then there are the hyphae, the threads&nbsp;
of the fungus that grow and lie in wait.

00:04:18.720 --> 00:04:25.120
Soon the rotifer will be more like this,&nbsp;
immobilized and ensnared in a cyst.

00:04:25.120 --> 00:04:31.680
The fungus’ digestive hyphae are embedded within&nbsp;
the rotifer’s body and are eating it from within.

00:04:31.680 --> 00:04:37.720
It takes only about a day for a fungus&nbsp;
to trap and consume its prey like this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.720 --> 00:04:42.080
but it’s still not done.

00:04:42.080 --> 00:04:46.680
Within another day, the rotifer will&nbsp;
essentially be filled with hyphae,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.680 --> 00:04:50.400
some even beginning to poke back&nbsp;
out through the body of its prey.

00:04:50.400 --> 00:04:53.800
The fungus has just been feeding and feeding.

00:04:53.800 --> 00:04:57.280
But still, it isn’t done.

00:04:57.280 --> 00:05:04.200
Within another day, the hyphae will produce&nbsp;
spores, planting the seed for the fungi’s future—

00:05:04.200 --> 00:05:09.960
all at the expense of the rotifer.

00:05:09.960 --> 00:05:15.360
In 1954, one scientist documenting the&nbsp;
various fungal attacks on rotifers within&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.360 --> 00:05:20.440
his lab noticed that they seemed&nbsp;
to have an unusual preference.

00:05:20.440 --> 00:05:25.200
They seemed to only target rotifers that&nbsp;
have a lorica, which is like a shell.

00:05:25.200 --> 00:05:28.880
The soft-bodied rotifers within&nbsp;
their samples seemed to be able&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.880 --> 00:05:32.320
to wander all around the fungi with no problem.

00:05:32.320 --> 00:05:34.160
That sounds wrong…right?

00:05:34.160 --> 00:05:40.160
Shells are protective, they keep you safe&nbsp;
from things that are trying to eat you.

00:05:40.160 --> 00:05:43.160
But the shells aren’t a deterrent to the fungi.

00:05:43.160 --> 00:05:44.880
Quite the opposite, in fact.

00:05:44.880 --> 00:05:50.160
The fungi seem to use the shell as&nbsp;
a place to hook their hyphae into,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.160 --> 00:05:55.520
using the rotifer’s shield&nbsp;
instead as a tool for invasion.

00:05:55.520 --> 00:05:58.400
These fungi also go after another type of rotifer:

00:05:58.400 --> 00:06:05.160
the bdelloid rotifers, a class of rotifers that&nbsp;
is fairly large, encompassing hundreds of species.

00:06:05.160 --> 00:06:08.680
These rotifers are known&nbsp;
for one very specific thing:

00:06:08.680 --> 00:06:12.240
no one has ever observed them having sex.

00:06:12.240 --> 00:06:14.240
It’s not like they’re shy or anything.

00:06:14.240 --> 00:06:18.640
They just don’t seem to do it…

00:06:18.640 --> 00:06:19.520
(maybe).

00:06:19.520 --> 00:06:22.200
There’s been some very recent&nbsp;
work sifting through their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.200 --> 00:06:27.600
genomes that suggests that maybe sexual&nbsp;
reproduction has shown up in their past.

00:06:27.600 --> 00:06:29.560
But that is a discussion for another day.

00:06:29.560 --> 00:06:32.320
For now, the fact remains that no one has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.320 --> 00:06:36.520
seen bdelloid rotifers actually&nbsp;
engaging in sexual reproduction.

00:06:39.040 --> 00:06:45.840
Even more curiously, no one has ever seen what&nbsp;
is confirmed to be a male bdelloid rotifer.

00:06:45.840 --> 00:06:51.200
There are, as far as we know,&nbsp;
only female bdelloid rotifers.

00:06:51.200 --> 00:06:53.440
They reproduce through parthenogenesis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.440 --> 00:06:59.720
able to make their own egg that will hatch into&nbsp;
a daughter who has only her mother’s genes.

00:06:59.720 --> 00:07:02.720
Scientists have wondered about&nbsp;
this process for some time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.720 --> 00:07:08.600
and how bdelloid rotifers have been able to&nbsp;
survive for millions and millions of years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.600 --> 00:07:13.120
without the advantages that sexual&nbsp;
reproduction confers on a species—

00:07:13.120 --> 00:07:18.040
like the ability to introduce variation that&nbsp;
could help them adapt to the world around them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.040 --> 00:07:21.440
a world that includes predatory fungi.

00:07:21.440 --> 00:07:26.640
But that’s also led scientists to wonder&nbsp;
if perhaps these fungi are the reason why&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.640 --> 00:07:32.200
bdelloid rotifers are so singularly&nbsp;
reliant on asexual reproduction.

00:07:32.200 --> 00:07:37.800
Because asexual reproduction has at least&nbsp;
one clear advantage on sexual reproduction:

00:07:37.800 --> 00:07:39.480
it’s faster.

00:07:39.480 --> 00:07:43.520
You don’t have to spend time finding&nbsp;
a mate or expend energy with all the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.520 --> 00:07:48.920
various processes involved with courting&nbsp;
and copulating that nature has engineered.

00:07:48.920 --> 00:07:54.920
That’s potentially useful to an animal&nbsp;
that finds itself on the run from a fungus.

00:07:54.920 --> 00:07:58.840
And bdelloid rotifers have another secret weapon.

00:07:58.840 --> 00:08:07.520
They are extremely hardy, able to turn into a&nbsp;
dormant tun that can survive extreme conditions.

00:08:07.520 --> 00:08:12.960
That means, they could survive something like&nbsp;
a strong wind, which could blow them away from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.960 --> 00:08:19.360
their predators and drop them off in a new&nbsp;
home that is hopefully a little less fungal.

00:08:19.360 --> 00:08:22.400
So this ability to outrun their predators and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.400 --> 00:08:26.040
quickly reproduce when they reach a&nbsp;
new, less threatening environment…

00:08:26.040 --> 00:08:31.840
that’s a potentially powerful combination,&nbsp;
one that could serve to explain why bdelloid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.840 --> 00:08:38.040
rotifers focus on asexual reproduction&nbsp;
at the expense of anything else.

00:08:38.040 --> 00:08:43.960
But it is, for now, still just one story for&nbsp;
why bdelloid rotifers live the way they do.

00:08:43.960 --> 00:08:51.200
The reality is probably even more complex&nbsp;
than what we know now, filled with more twists&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.200 --> 00:08:59.200
than the fungus that hunts this rotifer&nbsp;
and more sequels than we’ll ever know.

